<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eldena Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eldena Abbey was a Cistercian monastery near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> on the Pomeranian coast, founded in 1199 under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaromar-i-of-rugen">Jaromar I of Rügen</a>. The monks cleared land, promoted German settlement and founded the salt-trading settlement that grew into Greifswald. The abbey was dissolved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, and its brick Gothic ruins were later painted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspar-david-friedrich">Caspar David Friedrich</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eldena-abbey.jpg" alt="Eldena Abbey" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harald909 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Klosterruine_Eldena.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>In the 12th century the Baltic coast south of the island of Rügen belonged to the Rani principality of Rügen, which was subject to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>. The Danish Cistercian house of Esrum Abbey had founded a daughter house at Dargun, west of Demmin, in 1172. When Dargun Abbey was destroyed in 1198 in fighting between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, Jaromar I, Prince of the Rani, whose wife was of the Danish royal house, offered the monks a new site at the mouth of the River Ryck. The site included profitable salt pans, and the new house was first called Hilda Abbey.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> confirmed the foundation in 1204, and the princes of Rügen endowed the abbey with extensive lands in the border country towards the County of Gützkow. The monastery grew wealthy from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> and was influential in the Christianisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Western Pomerania</a>. Through the 13th century it organised the settlement of its estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>, founding Wendish, Danish and German villages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> began as its trading settlement opposite the salt pans, near the point where the via regia crossed the river.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bornhoved">Battle of Bornhöved</a> in 1227 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> withdrew, and in 1248/49 Duke Wartislaw III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> pressed the abbey into subinfeudating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a> to him. Wartislaw and later members of the House of Pomerania were buried in the abbey. Its influence in the town waned after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> and Dominicans founded friaries there. The abbey was dissolved in 1535, when Duke Philip I introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and took over its estates. The buildings were badly damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-greifswald">University of Greifswald</a> in 1634 and stripped of bricks for fortifications under Swedish rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Only ruins survive, all in Brick Gothic: the east end of the church from about 1200, conventual buildings of the 13th and 14th centuries, and the west front and nave of the 15th century. Restoration began in 1828, and a park was laid out on designs by Peter Joseph Lenné. Excavations of 1926-1927 traced the layout of the site, which is now a cultural site of the Pomerania Euroregion and is used for public events.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eldena Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the late twelfth century the dukes, monasteries and nobles invited German settlers, and the Cistercian abbeys of Kołbacz (Kolbatz) and <b>Eldena</b> helped clear and organise land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They founded monasteries such as Bergen and <b>Eldena</b> and invited German settlers, so Low German gradually replaced the Slavic speech of the Rani.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-rugen">Principality of Rügen</a></li></ul></section>
